{
  "schema": 1,
  "grade": "5",
  "band": "3-5",
  "focus": "Grade 5 is the year of nuance and of fair argument. Students learn that two words with the same dictionary meaning can feel different (frugal and stingy), that language can say one thing and mean another (proverbs, puns, symbols), and that a viewpoint needs proof and a logical case. Units introduce the language of debate (argument, viewpoint, proof, logical, trustworthy), the vocabulary of research and writing from sources (research, excerpt, quotation, glossary), and larger abstract concepts (dignity, conviction, resentment, composure). Morphology shifts from affixes toward Greek combining forms and prefix families that span science and social studies (tele-, micro-, scope, phon, aqua, bio, therm, graph, anti-, post-, semi-, super-, trans-). Compared with grade 4, words are more abstract and carry attitude; compared with grade 6, students still work in concrete, school-relevant contexts and the Tier 3 terms are named.",
  "selection": "Candidates were Tier 2 words that appear in grade 5-7 informational and literary texts and test prompts, weighted toward words that (a) carry connotation or contrast with a close neighbor, (b) belong to a family or share a root, and (c) let students argue, describe mood, and name character flaws precisely. Very frequent words and those already assigned to grades K-4 were excluded. Because the grade 6-8 scope claims the core analysis, argument, and rhetoric vocabulary (claim, persuade, valid, justify, analyze, evaluate, interpret, metaphor, hyperbole, democracy, hypothesis, conflict, compromise), grade 5 teaches the first-level versions (argument, debate, proof, logical; research, quotation; proverb, pun; economy, ballot) and leaves those words to later years. Disciplinary units name their Tier 3 words (predator, prey, erosion; economy, supply, demand; probability, experiment), never more than three per unit. Spanish cognates are favored where they exist (telescope/telescopio, symphony/sinfonia, probability/probabilidad, experiment/experimento, argument/argumento).",
  "arc": [
    "Positive or Negative?",
    "Proof, Viewpoint, and Debate",
    "Far, Small, Sound, Sight",
    "Standing Up",
    "Science: Food Chains and Fragile Land",
    "More Than Words",
    "Looking Back",
    "Water, Life, Heat, Writing",
    "Pride, Flattery, and Trickery",
    "Social Studies: Votes, Taxes, and Trade",
    "How Much? How Often?",
    "Setting the Mood",
    "Rivals and Allies",
    "Make It Simple: -ify, -ize, -ity, -ence/-ance",
    "Surviving Extremes",
    "Research and Writing from Sources",
    "Wonder and the Unknown",
    "Across the Curriculum: anti-, post-, semi-, super-, trans-",
    "Math and Science: Chance, Tests, and Tallies",
    "Feelings with Layers"
  ],
